# .gitignore for a Django project
# Purpose: sensible defaults for local development with Python, Django and VSCode.
# Keep important project files under version control (e.g. migrations), but exclude
# transient, build or machine-specific files (virtualenvs, caches, secrets, etc.).

# === Python bytecode and caches ===
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Cache and settings for popular tools
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.ruff_cache/

# === Virtual environments ===
# Don't commit virtual environments — developers should create their own.
venv/
ENV/
env/
.venv/

# If you use pipenv or poetry lockfiles, you may want to keep those files tracked.
# Pipenv lockfile: Pipfile.lock (tracked) — not ignored by default.

# === Environment / secret files ===
# Store secrets (API keys, DB passwords, etc.) in env files or a secrets manager —
# do NOT commit these to git.
.env
.env.*
!/.env
local_settings.py

# === Django specific ===
# SQLite (local dev database)
db.sqlite3
db.sqlite3-journal

# Media uploads (user-generated files) — usually kept out of repo
media/

# Collected static files (created by `collectstatic`) — do not commit
staticfiles/
# If your project uses a different STATIC_ROOT name, add it here
# static/

# Keep migrations themselves in version control so schema changes are tracked.
# We only ignore compiled caches inside migrations.
*/migrations/__pycache__/

# Django logs
*.log
logs/

# === Editor / IDE files ===
# VSCode workspace settings and local state — usually machine-specific.
.vscode/
# If you want to share recommended settings, instead commit a specific file like
# .vscode/settings.shared.json and add a small README explaining how to adopt them.

# JetBrains IDEs
.idea/

# macOS and Windows extras
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# === Build / distribution ===
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# === Node / frontend (if you have a frontend in the repo) ===
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# === Logs and runtime files ===
*.pid
*.seed
*.pid.lock

# === Celery ===
celerybeat-schedule
celerybeat.pid

# === Documentation build artifacts ===
docs/_build/

# === Optional: IDE or tooling exceptions ===
# If you want to keep some VSCode files under version control (for example a
# shared launch configuration or formatting rules), you can ignore everything
# in .vscode but then "un-ignore" specific files by adding lines like:
# !.vscode/settings.json
# !.vscode/launch.json

# === Helpful notes ===
# 1) Keep migrations tracked. They should not be ignored because they record
#    database schema history; ignoring them causes team sync problems.
# 2) Avoid committing any file that contains secrets (credentials, keys, tokens).
# 3) If you deploy through CI/CD, make sure CI builds re-create virtualenvs and
#    do not depend on files excluded here.
# 4) Review this file for your project's specifics (custom static/media root,
#    different virtualenv path, extra tools) and adjust accordingly.
